 - W01: before: Model of a body-cell cycle. Simplified; not to scale. The SVG used word boxes or an arrow/list model. After: Body-cell division: copied chromosomes separate, then one cell splits into two. Simplified; not to scale. Reason: D3 requires a drawn mechanism; arrows are reserved for real sequences. Source for the new labels: Pearson Edexcel GCSE Biology 1BI0 and Combined Science 1SC0, specification, Biology Topics 2–3; https://qualifications.pearson.com/content/dam/pdf/GCSE/Science/2016/Specification/gcse-biology-spec.pdf; https://qualifications.pearson.com/content/dam/pdf/GCSE/Science/2016/Specification/gcse-combinedscience-spec.pdf
 - W02: before: Growth routes in animal tissue and a plant shoot. Simplified; not to scale. The SVG used word boxes or an arrow/list model. After: An embryonic stem cell can give rise to different specialised cells; meristems at growing plant tips produce new cells. Simplified; not to scale. Reason: D3 requires a drawn mechanism; arrows are reserved for real sequences. Source for the new labels: Pearson Edexcel GCSE Biology 1BI0 and Combined Science 1SC0, specification, Biology Topics 2–3; https://qualifications.pearson.com/content/dam/pdf/GCSE/Science/2016/Specification/gcse-biology-spec.pdf; https://qualifications.pearson.com/content/dam/pdf/GCSE/Science/2016/Specification/gcse-combinedscience-spec.pdf; NHS Blood and Transplant, stem cell–derived red blood cells, https://www.nhsbt.nhs.uk/how-we-help/advanced-therapies/
 - W03: before: Fictional stem-cell proposal review. Simplified teaching frame; no medical decision is made here. The SVG used word boxes or an arrow/list model. After: Fictional proposal: weigh a possible benefit and risk against evidence. Simplified decision aid; no medical decision is made here. Reason: D3 requires a drawn mechanism; arrows are reserved for real sequences. Source for the new labels: Health Research Authority, protecting participants, https://www.hra.nhs.uk/about-us/what-we-do/our-role-protecting-research-participants/
-- W04: before: A gene is a DNA section; chromosomes package DNA; the genome is the whole DNA set. Simplified; not to scale. The SVG used word boxes or an arrow/list model. After: A chromosome is packaged DNA in a cell nucleus; a gene is a section of DNA. Zoom frames show parts within parts. Simplified; not to scale. Reason: D3 requires a drawn mechanism; arrows are reserved for real sequences. Source for the new labels: Wellcome Connecting Science, DNA in the cell, https://www.yourgenome.org/explore-genomics/in-the-cell/; NHGRI, Chromosomes Fact Sheet, https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/fact-sheets/Chromosomes-Fact-Sheet
+- W04: before: A gene is a DNA section; chromosomes package DNA; the genome is the whole DNA set. Simplified; not to scale. The SVG used word boxes or an arrow/list model. After: The genome is the full DNA set; a chromosome carries part of it; a gene is a section of DNA on a chromosome. Nested view; not to scale. Reason: D3 requires a drawn mechanism; arrows are reserved for real sequences. Source for the new labels: Wellcome Connecting Science, DNA in the cell, https://www.yourgenome.org/explore-genomics/in-the-cell/; NHGRI, Chromosomes Fact Sheet, https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/fact-sheets/Chromosomes-Fact-Sheet
 - W05: before: Invented flower allele model. Simplified; the four grid cells show possible combinations, not a prediction for a real family. The SVG used word boxes or an arrow/list model. After: Invented A/a flower model: Aa × aa gives two Aa and two aa possibilities. Each square is a possibility, not a family count. Simplified. Reason: D3 requires a drawn mechanism; arrows are reserved for real sequences. Source for the new labels: Pearson Edexcel GCSE Biology 1BI0 and Combined Science 1SC0, specification, Biology Topics 2–3; https://qualifications.pearson.com/content/dam/pdf/GCSE/Science/2016/Specification/gcse-biology-spec.pdf; https://qualifications.pearson.com/content/dam/pdf/GCSE/Science/2016/Specification/gcse-combinedscience-spec.pdf
 - W06: before: A source and inheritance model; simplified, not a prediction for a person. The SVG used word boxes or an arrow/list model. After: Simplified inheritance model: each parent carries one changed CFTR copy. The four grid squares are possibilities for each pregnancy, not a prediction for a person. Reason: D3 requires a drawn mechanism; arrows are reserved for real sequences. Source for the new labels: NHS, Cystic fibrosis, https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/cystic-fibrosis/; NHS Genomics Education Programme, https://www.genomicseducation.hee.nhs.uk/genotes/knowledge-hub/cystic-fibrosis/
 - W07: before: Three reminders for rehearsal, not answers to the independent check. Simplified; not to scale. The SVG used word boxes or an arrow/list model. After: Three separate rehearsal pictures: mitosis, a gene on DNA, and a Punnett square. Simplified; not to scale; not answers to the independent check. Reason: D3 requires a drawn mechanism; arrows are reserved for real sequences. Source for the new labels: Pearson Edexcel GCSE Biology 1BI0 and Combined Science 1SC0, specification, Biology Topics 2–3; https://qualifications.pearson.com/content/dam/pdf/GCSE/Science/2016/Specification/gcse-biology-spec.pdf; https://qualifications.pearson.com/content/dam/pdf/GCSE/Science/2016/Specification/gcse-combinedscience-spec.pdf
 
 No other factual claims were changed. One sourced diagram-claim row was added to each facts ledger.
