inherit;border:2px solid #295d70;background:#fff;color:#102638;border-radius:6px;padding:.35rem .6rem;max-width:100%}button{cursor:pointer}.feedback{min-height:1.7rem;font-weight:bold}.diagram{margin:.7rem 0;padding:.5rem;border:2px solid #557685;background:#f2f7f8;border-radius:10px}.diagram svg{width:100%;height:auto;max-height:160px}.diagram figcaption{font-size:.92rem}.card{border:2px solid #557685;padding:.7rem;border-radius:8px;background:#f2f7f8}[data-mbm-guide]{display:none!important}@media(max-width:390px){body{font-size:1rem}header,main,footer{padding:.7rem}.stage{padding:.8rem}h1{font-size:1.7rem}.goal{font-size:1.1rem}.wordtable{font-size:.84rem}.wordtable th,.wordtable td{padding:.2rem;overflow-wrap:anywhere}}@media(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){*{animation:none!important;transition:none!important;scroll-behavior:auto!important}}@media print{@page{size:A4;margin:12mm}body{background:#fff;font-size:10pt;color:#000}header{color:#000;background:#fff}.stage{break-inside:avoid;margin:.45rem 0;padding:.5rem}.diagram{break-inside:avoid}header a{color:#000}}@media print{@page{size:A4;margin:12mm}main{border:2px solid #123e52;padding:5mm}.diagram svg{max-height:120px}}</style></head><body><header><a href="../../../index.html">← Lessons</a><a href="#organiser">Skip to organiser</a><p>LAUNCH Science Autumn 2 W5</p><h1>A simple genetic cross</h1></header><main id="organiser" tabindex="-1"><p class="goal">I am learning to calculate genetic probability with a Punnett square and evaluate its limits.</p><h2>Words to use</h2><table class="wordtable"><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th><th>My language</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">allele</th><td>a version of a gene</td><td>A and a represent two alleles in our invented model.</td><td aria-label="Write your own language word here">&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">genotype</th><td>the pair of alleles shown by letters</td><td>Aa is one possible genotype in this model.</td><td aria-label="Write your own language word here">&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">dominant</th><td>an allele shown by one copy in this simple model</td><td>A is dominant in our invented flower model.</td><td aria-label="Write your own language word here">&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">recessive</th><td>an allele shown when two copies are present in this model</td><td>aa shows the recessive version in our model.</td><td aria-label="Write your own language word here">&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Punnett square</th><td>a grid that shows possible allele pairs</td><td>The Punnett square for Aa × aa has four cells.</td><td aria-label="Write your own language word here">&nbsp;</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Model</h2><figure class="diagram"><svg id="punnett-model" viewBox="0 0 420 235" role="img" aria-label="An Aa parent has A and a across the top. An aa parent has a and a down the side. Four drawn grid cells read Aa, aa, Aa and aa."><title>Drawn Punnett square</title><text x="215" y="25" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="18" fill="#17394a" >Aa parent</text><text x="104" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" fill="#17394a" >aa parent</text><rect x="172" y="56" width="132" height="132" rx="2" fill="#fff" stroke="#17394a" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M238 56v132M172 122h132" fill="none" stroke="#17394a" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" /><text x="205.0" y="46" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >A</text><text x="271.0" y="46" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >a</text><text x="156" y="94.28" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >a</text><text x="156" y="160.28" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >a</text><text x="205.0" y="96.92" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >Aa</text><text x="271.0" y="96.92" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >aa</text><text x="205.0" y="162.92000000000002" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >Aa</text><text x="271.0" y="162.92000000000002" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >aa</text><text x="237" y="218" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="16" fill="#17394a" >four possible pairs</text></svg><figcaption>Invented A/a flower model: Aa × aa gives two Aa and two aa possibilities. Each square is a possibility, not a family count. Simplified.</figcaption></figure><p>Place A/a atop the grid for one parent; place a/a at its side for the other. Pair one allele from each edge: Aa, aa, Aa, aa. Two of four boxes are aa: a 1 in 2 chance per offspring, not an exact family count.</p><h2>How I know</h2><ul><li>I can pair one allele from each parent.</li><li>I can find the aa chance in four squares.</li><li>I can evaluate why probability does not guarantee an exact count.</li></ul><p>You can plan in any language. Speak before you write: The chance is … because …; this does not promise …</p></main><footer><p>Print one A4 sheet or read it on a screen.</p></footer></body></html>
