id #295d70;background:#fff;color:#102638;border-radius:6px;padding:.35rem .6rem;max-width:100%}button{cursor:pointer}.feedback{min-height:1.7rem;font-weight:bold}.diagram{margin:.7rem 0;padding:.5rem;border:2px solid #557685;background:#f2f7f8;border-radius:10px}.diagram svg{width:100%;height:auto;max-height:160px}.diagram figcaption{font-size:.92rem}.card{border:2px solid #557685;padding:.7rem;border-radius:8px;background:#f2f7f8}[data-mbm-guide]{display:none!important}@media(max-width:390px){body{font-size:1rem}header,main,footer{padding:.7rem}.stage{padding:.8rem}h1{font-size:1.7rem}.goal{font-size:1.1rem}.wordtable{font-size:.84rem}.wordtable th,.wordtable td{padding:.2rem;overflow-wrap:anywhere}}@media(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){*{animation:none!important;transition:none!important;scroll-behavior:auto!important}}@media print{@page{size:A4;margin:12mm}body{background:#fff;font-size:10pt;color:#000}header{color:#000;background:#fff}.stage{break-inside:avoid;margin:.45rem 0;padding:.5rem}.diagram{break-inside:avoid}header a{color:#000}}</style></head><body><header><a href="../../../index.html">← Lessons</a><a href="#lesson">Skip to lesson</a><p>LAUNCH Science Autumn 2 W5</p><h1>A simple genetic cross</h1><p class="goal">I am learning to calculate genetic probability with a Punnett square and evaluate its limits.</p><p>40 minutes. You may pass on a personal example.</p></header><main id="lesson" tabindex="-1"><p data-mbm-guide>Verbatim SoW objective: Complete a genetic cross (digital Punnett square).</p><section class="stage" id="stage-arrival"><h2>Arrival <span class="meta">· 5 min</span></h2><p>Look at this week’s letter tiles A and a. Point to one detail you notice.</p><figure class="diagram"><svg id="punnett-model" viewBox="0 0 420 235" role="img" aria-label="An Aa parent has A and a across the top. An aa parent has a and a down the side. Four drawn grid cells read Aa, aa, Aa and aa."><title>Drawn Punnett square</title><text x="215" y="25" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="18" fill="#17394a" >Aa parent</text><text x="104" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" fill="#17394a" >aa parent</text><rect x="172" y="56" width="132" height="132" rx="2" fill="#fff" stroke="#17394a" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M238 56v132M172 122h132" fill="none" stroke="#17394a" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" /><text x="205.0" y="46" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >A</text><text x="271.0" y="46" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >a</text><text x="156" y="94.28" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >a</text><text x="156" y="160.28" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >a</text><text x="205.0" y="96.92" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >Aa</text><text x="271.0" y="96.92" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >aa</text><text x="205.0" y="162.92000000000002" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >Aa</text><text x="271.0" y="162.92000000000002" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" fill="#17394a" >aa</text><text x="237" y="218" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="16" fill="#17394a" >four possible pairs</text></svg><figcaption>Invented A/a flower model: Aa × aa gives two Aa and two aa possibilities. Each square is a possibility, not a family count. Simplified.</figcaption></figure><p data-mbm-guide>Offer a private, predictable start. Do not require previous knowledge.</p></section><section class="stage" id="stage-starter"><h2>Starter <span class="meta">· 3 min</span></h2><p>Recall three ideas from earlier lessons. Show one answer at a time.</p><ol><li>What is a gene?</li><li>What do stem cells do in growth?</li><li>Where are chromosomes found in an animal cell?</li></ol><p><strong>Today I can:</strong></p><ul><li>I can pair one allele from each parent.</li><li>I can find the aa chance in four squares.</li><li>I can evaluate why probability does not guarantee an exact count.</li></ul><p data-mbm-guide>All pupils respond; reveal the answers only after attempts. Revisit success criteria.</p></section><section class="stage" id="stage-i_do"><h2>I do <span class="meta">· 5 min</span></h2><p>Watch the full model. Point to the step that answers today’s question.</p><h3>Words</h3><table class="wordtable"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Word</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">allele</th><td>a version of a gene</td><td>A and a represent two alleles in our invented model.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">genotype</th><td>the pair of alleles shown by letters</td><td>Aa is one possible genotype in this model.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">dominant</th><td>an allele shown by one copy in this simple model</td><td>A is dominant in our invented flower model.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">recessive</th><td>an allele shown when two copies are present in this model</td><td>aa shows the recessive version in our model.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Punnett square</th><td>a grid that shows possible allele pairs</td><td>The Punnett square for Aa × aa has four cells.</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="card"><strong>Full model:</strong> Place A/a atop the grid for one parent; place a/a at its side for the other. Pair one allele from each edge: Aa, aa, Aa, aa. Two of four boxes are aa: a 1 in 2 chance per offspring, not an exact family count.</p><p><strong>Show me:</strong> Show me: which allele can the aa parent pass on?</p><p data-mbm-guide>Answer: Only a.</p><p data-mbm-guide>Model one complete example; check every pupil’s response to the show-me question.</p></section><section class="stage" id="stage-we_do"><h2>We do <span class="meta">· 7 min</span></h2><p>Complete the missing step in the part-done model. Speak, then match the cards.</p><p class="reset">One-minute reset: use the agreed move, then return to one step.</p><p><strong>Part-done model:</strong> Aa × aa: top A, a; side a, a. First row Aa, aa; second row ___, ___. Chance of aa: ___ out of 4.</p><p><strong>Speak first:</strong> One allele from the top is …; one from the side is …; together they make …</p><p class="way">F · support: Place one letter tile from the top and one from the side into a single square. Point to the next pair.</p><p class="tier">M · explain: Explain why the two aa squares represent half the model outcomes.</p><p class="deeper">H · stretch: Predict the aa chance for Aa × Aa before checking a second model. Explain the change.</p><div class="sort"><h3>Complete the 2 × 2 digital grid. Choose the genotype in each square.</h3><form id="match-form"><table class="wordtable"><caption>Digital Punnett square: one allele from each parent per cell</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">aa × Aa</th><th scope="col">A</th><th scope="col">a</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">a</th><td data-answer="Aa"><label>Row 1, A with a <select aria-label="Row 1, A with a genotype"><option value="">Choose…</option><option value="Aa">Aa</option><option value="aa">aa</option></select><output aria-live="polite"></output></label></td><td data-answer="aa"><label>Row 1, a with a <select aria-label="Row 1, a with a genotype"><option value="">Choose…</option><option value="Aa">Aa</option><option value="aa">aa</option></select><output aria-live="polite"></output></label></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">a</th><td data-answer="Aa"><label>Row 2, A with a <select aria-label="Row 2, A with a genotype"><option value="">Choose…</option><option value="Aa">Aa</option><option value="aa">aa</option></select><output aria-live="polite"></output></label></td><td data-answer="aa"><label>Row 2, a with a <select aria-label="Row 2, a with a genotype"><option value="">Choose…</option><option value="Aa">Aa</option><option value="aa">aa</option></select><output aria-live="polite"></output></label></td></tr></tbody></table></form><button type="button" id="match-check">Check the grid</button><button type="button" id="match-reset">Start again</button><p class="feedback" id="match-feedback" role="status" aria-live="polite">Ready to match.</p></div><p data-mbm-guide>Wait five seconds, gesture, then cue if needed. Support the route without supplying the missing answer.</p></section><section class="stage" id="stage-we_do_2"><h2>We do 2 <span class="meta">· 5 min</span></h2><p>Choose one hinge answer. Show it at the same time as the group.</p><div class="hinge"><p><strong>All-pupil hinge:</strong> In the invented Aa × aa model, what is the chance of an aa offspring?</p><form><fieldset><legend>Choose one answer</legend><label><input type="radio" name="hinge-SCI_LAUNCH_A2_W05" value="A">A. 1 in 2.</label><label><input type="radio" name="hinge-SCI_LAUNCH_A2_W05" value="B">B. 1 in 4.</label><label><input type="radio" name="hinge-SCI_LAUNCH_A2_W05" value="C">C. Certain: every offspring is aa.</label><label><input type="radio" name="hinge-SCI_LAUNCH_A2_W05" value="D">D. Impossible: no parent has a.</label></fieldset><button type="reset">Clear choice</button></form><p>Show one answer to the adult at the same time as everyone else. Point or use a card, too. Then explain to a partner.</p></div><p data-mbm-guide>Collect simultaneous responses, apply the decision rule and parallel hinge in the staff note.</p></section><section class="stage" id="stage-independent"><h2>Independent <span class="meta">· 8 min</span></h2><p>Calculate the aa probability for Aa × aa. Evaluate an exact-count claim about real offspring.</p><p class="reset">One-minute reset: use the agreed move, then return to one step.</p><p><strong>Talk before write:</strong> The chance is … because …; this does not promise …</p><p>You can plan in any language.</p><p class="tier"><strong>F:</strong> Complete Aa × aa with allele tiles. <em>Top ___ and side ___ make ___.</em></p><p class="tier"><strong>M:</strong> Calculate and justify the aa probability from the grid. <em>I count ___ aa squares out of four, so …</em></p><p class="tier"><strong>H:</strong> Evaluate an exact-count claim about four real offspring. <em>The grid shows a chance, not a promise, because …</em></p><p class="deeper">Go deeper: Compare aa chances for Aa × Aa and Aa × aa with a second grid.</p><p class="deeper">Early-finisher stretch — Faulty headings audit: Correct a faulty grid that gives an Aa parent two A headings; explain its wrong probability.</p><p><strong>Show it your way:</strong> point, sign, speak, draw, type, write, use a scribe or a staff-captured photo.</p><p data-mbm-guide>At lesson start choose one adult with supported pupils and one holding the group; swap during the week. Record independence and prompt level.</p></section><section class="stage" id="stage-review"><h2>Review <span class="meta">· 4 min</span></h2><p>Tell a partner what you changed after feedback. Mark a real edit // and read it back when asked ?.</p><p class="reset">One-minute reset: use the agreed move, then return to one step.</p><p><strong>My feedback was …</strong> Name the actual point an adult or partner noticed. Make one true self-edit <strong>//</strong>. Ask “Read this back to me” <strong>?</strong> if that helps.</p><p data-mbm-guide>Give a precise VF or NS(+) only when it actually happens. Invite a true self-edit and read-back.</p></section><section class="stage" id="stage-exit"><h2>Exit <span class="meta">· 3 min</span></h2><p>Show what you know with a model and explain one reason.</p><p><strong>Check today’s success:</strong></p><ul><li>I can pair one allele from each parent.</li><li>I can find the aa chance in four squares.</li><li>I can evaluate why probability does not guarantee an exact count.</li></ul><p><strong>What am I learning?</strong> I am learning to calculate genetic probability with a Punnett square and evaluate its limits.</p><p><strong>My feedback was …</strong> Name what you heard at Review.</p><p><strong>Next I need to …</strong> Check the top and side alleles before giving a chance.</p><p><strong>How I know:</strong> I can fill Aa × aa and explain that two of four model squares are aa, with no promise about real offspring.</p><p>Your teacher hears your answer and uses it to choose our next model, prompt or example.</p><p data-mbm-guide>Hear the answer privately in a valid mode and use it to change the next example or prompt.</p></section></main><footer><p>Offline lesson · no answers or scores are stored.</p></footer><script>"use strict";(()=>{const form=document.getElementById('match-form'),out=document.getElementById('match-feedback');document.getElementById('match-check').addEventListener('click',()=>{let blanks=0,good=0;form.querySelectorAll('select').forEach(s=>{const answer=s.closest('[data-answer]').dataset.answer;if(!s.value){blanks++;s.nextElementSibling.textContent='Choose a match.'}else if(s.value===answer){good++;s.nextElementSibling.textContent='That fits. Explain why.'}else s.nextElementSibling.textContent='Check the model and try again.'});out.textContent=blanks?'Choose a match for each card.':good===form.querySelectorAll('select').length?'All matched. Tell a partner your reason.':'Use the model to repair a match.'});document.getElementById('match-reset').addEventListener('click',()=>{form.reset();form.querySelectorAll('output').forEach(x=>x.textContent='');out.textContent='Ready to match.';form.querySelector('select').focus()});})();</script></body></html>
